"""Reference solution for the in-place ``renorm_`` operator (operator 849).

Frozen Definition ``aten::renorm_(Tensor(a!) self, Scalar p, int dim,
Scalar maxnorm) -> Tensor(a!)`` declares
``effects.mutates == ["self"]`` and ``effects.returns_alias_of == {"out": "self"}``,
so the callable has to renormalize the caller's storage in place and hand back
the very same Tensor object (no new storage, no copy-out).

The oracle delegates to the same native ATen kernel that the frozen master
tests already use on their expected side.  It imports no FlagGems module and
never resolves ``flag_gems.renorm_``, so the injected candidate can never serve
as its own oracle.  dtype, device, empty-input, non-contiguous and
invalid-argument behavior are inherited from that native call instead of being
re-implemented here, so no new ABI, rejection rule or autograd behavior is
invented.
"""

import torch

__all__ = ["run", "renorm_"]


def run(self, p, dim, maxnorm):
    """Renormalize ``self`` along ``dim`` in place and return the same tensor."""
    torch.ops.aten.renorm_(self, p, dim, maxnorm)
    # ``aten.renorm_`` writes into ``self`` and returns it; returning the input
    # object explicitly keeps the frozen alias/identity contract observable
    # (``res_out is inp`` and an unchanged ``data_ptr()``).
    return self


# Same callable exposed under the operator name, so an override spec that omits
# the explicit function name (``renorm_:<file>``) still resolves to this
# implementation; its code object name stays the declared entrypoint ``run``.
renorm_ = run
